## CSS Preprocessors

A preprocessor adds programming constructs to CSS and compiles the result to standard CSS that browsers understand.

## Key Features (Sass/SCSS example)

```scss
// Variables
$primary: #0070f3;
$radius: 4px;

// Nesting
.card {
  border-radius: $radius;

  &:hover { box-shadow: 0 4px 12px rgba(0,0,0,.1); }

  &__title { font-size: 1.25rem; }
}

// Mixin (reusable block)
@mixin flex-center {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
}

.hero { @include flex-center; }

// Partials — split across files, imported into one
@use 'tokens/colors';
@use 'components/button';
```

## Why Use One?

- **DRY** — define a color or spacing value once, reuse everywhere.
- **Nesting** — keeps component styles grouped and readable.
- **Partials** — split large stylesheets into maintainable files.
- **Functions and loops** — generate utility classes or theme scales programmatically.

## Modern Alternative

With native CSS custom properties (`--variable`) and `@layer`, many preprocessor benefits are now available without a build step. Tailwind CSS takes a different approach: utility classes generated at build time. Preprocessors remain common in legacy codebases and component libraries.

## Sass Overview

Sass is compiled by the Dart Sass compiler (the current official implementation) into plain CSS.

## Two Syntaxes

```sass
// .sass (indented — no braces or semicolons)
.button
  background: blue
  &:hover
    background: darkblue
```

```scss
// .scss (superset of CSS — most common)
.button {
  background: blue;
  &:hover { background: darkblue; }
}
```

## Core Features

```scss
// @use — module system (replaces @import)
@use 'sass:math';
@use 'tokens' as t;

// Variables
$spacing-4: 1rem;

// Mixins with arguments
@mixin respond-to($bp) {
  @media (min-width: $bp) { @content; }
}

.hero {
  padding: $spacing-4;
  @include respond-to(768px) { padding: $spacing-4 * 2; }
}

// @extend — share a set of declarations
%visually-hidden {
  position: absolute;
  width: 1px;
  height: 1px;
  overflow: hidden;
  clip-path: inset(50%);
}
.sr-only { @extend %visually-hidden; }
```

## Current Status

Sass is still widely used in large codebases and UI libraries. However, many modern projects favour CSS custom properties + PostCSS or Tailwind, which reduce the need for a full preprocessor.

## CSS Floats

`float: left | right | none` removes an element from normal flow and shifts it to the side, letting inline content wrap around it.

```css
/* Classic image float */
.article img {
  float: left;
  margin: 0 1rem 1rem 0;
}
```

## The Collapse Problem

Because floated elements are out of normal flow, a parent containing only floated children collapses to zero height:

```html
<div class="container">        <!-- collapses! -->
  <div style="float:left">A</div>
  <div style="float:left">B</div>
</div>
```

## Clearfix Solutions

```css
/* Modern clearfix */
.clearfix::after {
  content: '';
  display: block;
  clear: both;
}

/* Alternative — overflow triggers a new BFC */
.container { overflow: hidden; }

/* Best alternative — just use Flexbox */
.container { display: flex; gap: 1rem; }
```

## When Floats Still Apply

Floats are still the correct tool for **text wrapping around images** — something Flexbox and Grid don't replicate. The `shape-outside` property even lets you wrap text around non-rectangular shapes:

```css
.pull-quote {
  float: left;
  shape-outside: circle(50%);
}
```

For page layouts, always prefer Flexbox or Grid.

## CSS Positioning

### static (default)

Element follows normal document flow. `top`, `left`, `right`, `bottom`, and `z-index` have **no effect**.

### relative

```css
.badge {
  position: relative;
  top: -4px;   /* offsets from its natural position */
               /* original space is still reserved */
}
```

Also creates a **positioning context** for absolutely positioned children.

### absolute

```css
.parent { position: relative; } /* establishes context */

.tooltip {
  position: absolute;
  top: 100%;   /* relative to .parent, not the viewport */
  left: 0;
               /* removed from flow — no space reserved */
}
```

If no positioned ancestor exists, it positions relative to the initial containing block (viewport).

### fixed

```css
.sticky-nav {
  position: fixed;
  top: 0;
  width: 100%;
  /* stays on screen during scroll */
}
```

### sticky

```css
th {
  position: sticky;
  top: 0;  /* acts relative until scrolled to this offset, then sticks */
}
```

## Key Rule

`absolute`, `fixed`, and `sticky` create a new **stacking context**. Always set a `position` other than `static` on a parent before using `absolute` children.

## Pseudo-Elements

Pseudo-elements target a specific **part** of an element rather than the element itself.

## ::before and ::after

Insert generated content as the first/last child. Requires `content` property (can be empty string).

```css
/* Decorative quote marks */
blockquote::before { content: '\201C'; font-size: 4rem; }
blockquote::after  { content: '\201D'; }

/* Clearfix */
.clearfix::after {
  content: '';
  display: block;
  clear: both;
}

/* Expand URLs for print */
@media print {
  a::after { content: ' (' attr(href) ')'; }
}
```

## ::first-line and ::first-letter

```css
p::first-letter {
  float: left;
  font-size: 3em;
  line-height: 1;
  margin-right: 0.1em;
}
```

## ::placeholder and ::selection

```css
input::placeholder { color: #999; font-style: italic; }

::selection { background: #0070f3; color: white; }
```

## ::marker (lists)

```css
li::marker {
  color: #0070f3;
  font-weight: bold;
}
```

## Pseudo-class vs Pseudo-element

| | Prefix | Targets |
|---|---|---|
| Pseudo-class | `:` | State or position (`:hover`, `:nth-child`) |
| Pseudo-element | `::` | Part of an element (`::before`, `::first-line`) |

## How the Browser Renders CSS

## Step-by-Step Pipeline

**1. Parse** — HTML → DOM, CSS → CSSOM (runs in parallel where possible).

**2. Style calculation** — browser matches every DOM node to its applicable CSS rules and computes a final style value for each property.

**3. Layout (Reflow)** — calculates exact geometry (size, position) for every render object, accounting for the box model, floats, Flexbox, Grid, etc.

**4. Paint** — fills in pixels: text, colors, borders, shadows. Chrome DevTools' Paint profiler shows which layers repaint.

**5. Compositing** — the browser assembles layers on the GPU. Elements with `transform`, `opacity`, or `will-change` get their own compositor layer.

## What Triggers Each Step

```
Change width/height   → Layout + Paint + Composite  (expensive)
Change color/shadow   → Paint + Composite            (medium)
Change transform/opacity → Composite only           (cheap, GPU)
```

## Practical Implications

```css
/* Bad — triggers layout on every frame */
.animate { animation: slide 0.3s; }
@keyframes slide { to { left: 100px; } }

/* Good — compositor-only */
.animate { animation: slide 0.3s; }
@keyframes slide { to { transform: translateX(100px); } }
```

Use `will-change: transform` sparingly to hint the browser to promote an element to its own layer before an animation starts.

## Reset vs Normalize

## CSS Reset

Aggressively strips browser defaults — margins, paddings, font sizes, list styles, everything:

```css
/* Eric Meyer's reset (simplified) */
* { margin: 0; padding: 0; box-sizing: border-box; }
ol, ul { list-style: none; }
a { color: inherit; text-decoration: none; }
```

**Pros:** Complete control from zero.  
**Cons:** You must re-declare every style, including sensible defaults like `<strong>` being bold.

## Normalize.css

Fixes cross-browser inconsistencies while keeping useful defaults intact:

```css
/* normalize.css approach */
button, input { font-family: inherit; } /* fix font inheritance */
pre { overflow: auto; }                 /* fix overflow in IE */
/* leaves h1–h6 sizes, list bullets, etc. intact */
```

**Pros:** Less work, readable output, actively maintained.  
**Cons:** Slightly larger file; you inherit some opinions.

## Modern Approach

Many teams use a **minimal custom base** that combines both ideas — fix browser quirks, reset a few noisy defaults, and use `box-sizing: border-box` universally:

```css
*, *::before, *::after { box-sizing: border-box; }
body { margin: 0; line-height: 1.5; -webkit-font-smoothing: antialiased; }
img, video { max-width: 100%; display: block; }
```

Next.js and many frameworks bundle a similar base by default.
